 The output is written to `cmake-build-rdp-protocol-provider/bin`.
 
-On a VM, copy `SunshineRdpProvider.dll`, `SunshineRdpProviderCheck.exe`, and the
-PowerShell scripts into the same directory, then run:
-
-```powershell
-powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\install_probe.ps1
-```
-
-Use `-RestartTermService` only when testing `TermService` loading. The script
-creates a new `WinStations\Sunshine-Idd` listener and does not change the
-existing `RDP-Tcp` listener.
-
-Win11 client and RDPWrap note: `IWRdsProtocolManager` is a server-side RDS
-provider API. Microsoft documents no supported client OS for this interface.
-The current Win11 25H2/RDPWrap probe can self-activate the COM object, but
-`TermService` does not call the provider and logs that the RD Session Host role
-is not installed. Validate the provider load path on Windows Server with the RD
-Session Host role before wiring the remote IDD hardware ID.
+## Current custom Remote IDD proof
+
+The current path uses the transparent RDP Core proxy to return
+`SUNSHINE_REMOTE_IDDCX`. `RemoteDisplayEnum` consequently creates a seat-scoped
+device which loads `SunshineRemoteDisplayDriver.dll`. The guarded session then
+runs `SunshineRdpRemoteHdrTransition.exe`, followed by the bounded
+`SunshineRdpDxgiHdrSourcePin.exe` helper. No private RdpIdd handle or monitor
+IOCTL participates in that transaction.
+
+The proof retains the account, listener, TermService compatibility, RunOnce,
+and proxy rollback guards. `SunshineRdpHdrProofControl.exe --assert-safe`
+remains the terminal safety check.
+
+## Archived experiments
+
+The private inbox-RdpIdd path, HMD publication variants, token bridge, BGRA8
+swapchain rejection, and one-off reverse-engineering probes are not part of the
+working design. Their standalone source, scripts, and captured artifacts are
+retained for evidence under `dev/artifacts/retired-inbox-rdpidd-20260815` and
+the Duo-session progress ledger. The normal build exposes no environment switch
+for the remaining guarded legacy branches in `provider.cpp`. Do not restore
+them to the active build or use their old live recipes.
+
+The important negative result is that issuing a private monitor IOCTL from the
+provider can block a TermService callback and stall Winlogon. The current path
+avoids that failure class entirely: the provider returns the project hardware
+ID, the custom IDD uses documented IddCx APIs, and HDR activation happens later
+from a bounded user-mode helper.
